+### Compound Durations
+
+`parseCompound` sums several `<number><unit>` parts. Parts can be separated by spaces, commas, or nothing.
+
+```ts
+import { parseCompound } from 'ms';
+
+parseCompound('1h 30m');         // 5400000
+parseCompound('1h, 30m');        // 5400000
+parseCompound('2 days 4 hours'); // 187200000
+```
+
+A single value (`'1h'`, `'100'`) works like `parse`. With multiple parts, every part needs a unit, so `'1h 30'` throws. Invalid input throws a `TypeError` instead of returning `NaN`.
+
+### Clock Strings
+
+`parseClock` parses timer-style strings: `m:ss` and `h:mm:ss`.
+
+```ts
+import { parseClock } from 'ms';
+
+parseClock('1:30');     // 90000
+parseClock('01:02:03'); // 3723000
+parseClock('1:60');     // undefined
+```
+
+Seconds (and minutes, when hours are present) must be two digits from `00` to `59`. Invalid input returns `undefined` instead of throwing, so check the result before using it.
+
 ### Enhanced Type Safety
 
 If you want strict type checking for the input value, you can use `parseStrict`.
